1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,389
The brush uncovers an altar of layered brick, laid out with real precision.

2
00:00:05,389 --> 00:00:11,596
Nothing is worshipped here that can be drawn: there is no image, no figure, only fire and geometry.

3
00:00:17,167 --> 00:00:24,035
This episode is about the order that grew up around that fire — an order that organised Indian society for a very long time,

4
00:00:24,035 --> 00:00:26,423
and that is still argued about today.

5
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:33,576
Mostly from texts, and that is important to say plainly.

6
00:00:33,576 --> 00:00:39,089
The texts tell us how society was supposed to be arranged, by the people doing the arranging.

7
00:00:39,089 --> 00:00:44,550
How far ordinary life matched that is a separate question, and the evidence is thinner.

8
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:53,678
The word varna names a fourfold order: brahmana, the priest and teacher; kshatriya,

9
00:00:53,678 --> 00:01:00,451
the ruler and warrior; vaishya, the farmer, herder and trader; and shudra, who serves the other three.

10
00:01:03,767 --> 00:01:06,354
The classic description appears in the Purusha Sukta,

11
00:01:06,354 --> 00:01:11,576
a hymn in the tenth book of the Rigveda — and that tenth book is generally held to be the latest part of it,

12
00:01:11,576 --> 00:01:13,553
which matters for dating the idea.

13
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:22,800
In the earliest layer the divisions look occupational and fairly loose:

14
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,980
the texts record people whose family members followed different callings.

15
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:29,935
By the later Vedic period the picture hardens.

16
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,891
Position is increasingly tied to birth, the top two groups claim ritual privileges,

17
00:01:37,891 --> 00:01:41,205
and the distance between the fourth group and the rest grows.

18
00:01:41,205 --> 00:01:45,836
The direction of travel, across these centuries, is from flexible towards fixed.

19
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:54,104
Alongside varna sits a second scheme: the ashramas, four stages of an individual life.

20
00:01:54,104 --> 00:01:56,112
Brahmacharya, the student.

21
00:01:56,112 --> 00:01:58,194
Grihastha, the householder.

22
00:01:58,194 --> 00:02:01,095
Vanaprastha, withdrawal to the forest.

23
00:02:01,095 --> 00:02:03,180
Sannyasa, renunciation.

24
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:13,866
And four aims of life, the purusharthas: dharma, right conduct; artha, material wellbeing; kama, pleasure and desire; moksha, release.

25
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:21,642
Note that this was an ideal scheme, set out in texts, not a description of how most people actually lived.

26
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:24,655
And women.

27
00:02:24,655 --> 00:02:34,120
In the early Vedic period women took part in rituals and in debate, and the texts name some of them: Gargi, who questions a teacher closely in the Upanishads;

28
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:41,266
Maitreyi, who asks what wealth is worth if it does not bring immortality; Lopamudra, to whom hymns are attributed.

29
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:49,932
In the later Vedic period the picture narrows: participation in sacrifice declines,

30
00:02:49,932 --> 00:02:54,306
access to Vedic study is restricted, and the household role is emphasised.

31
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:58,339
Both things are true, and the change between them is the point.

32
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:02,680
How fixed was this order to begin with?

33
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:09,604
One reading holds that varna started as a loose division of work and hardened over centuries into something determined by birth.

34
00:03:09,604 --> 00:03:12,073
Another holds that hierarchy was there early,

35
00:03:12,073 --> 00:03:17,257
and that we should be cautious with texts composed by the group placed at the top of them.

36
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:24,546
This is a live and consequential debate.

37
00:03:24,546 --> 00:03:29,621
What is not in dispute is the direction: the order became more rigid over time, and jati,

38
00:03:29,621 --> 00:03:33,569
the much larger system of birth-groups most people actually lived in,

39
00:03:33,569 --> 00:03:37,930
develops later and is a different thing from the fourfold varna scheme.

40
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,936
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

41
00:03:44,936 --> 00:03:46,583
Four stages of a life.

42
00:03:46,583 --> 00:03:48,387
Four aims of a life.

43
00:03:52,467 --> 00:03:53,145
Dig Quiz.

44
00:03:53,145 --> 00:03:54,433
Pause and name it.

45
00:03:54,433 --> 00:04:00,806
First: the hymn in the tenth book of the Rigveda that gives the classic fourfold description.

46
00:04:00,806 --> 00:04:02,395
The Purusha Sukta.

47
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:08,987
Second: the four stages of an individual life, from student to renunciant.

48
00:04:08,987 --> 00:04:09,837
The ashramas.

49
00:04:09,837 --> 00:04:16,876
Third: the woman in the Upanishads who asks whether wealth is worth anything if it does not bring immortality.

50
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:19,121
Maitreyi.

51
00:04:19,121 --> 00:04:24,634
In Mains, a standing question asks you to trace the development of the varna order.

52
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:29,524
Trace it: occupational and loose, then increasingly by birth,

53
00:04:29,524 --> 00:04:34,457
and say clearly which parts are textual ideal and which are evidenced practice.

54
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:43,630
A fire altar takes its place in the gallery: the centre of a world with no temples and no images.

55
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:52,155
Next gallery: south India, where a completely different world was burying its dead under stone.

56
00:04:52,155 --> 00:04:56,010
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

